    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-346717",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,1)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-1,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=1,$ estimate the value of $f(-0.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n\n$\\,f(-0.3)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1),$ so we have $f(0)=1.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =1+1(x-0) \\\\  & =1+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(-0.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-0.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.3) & ≈L(-0.3) \\\\  & =1+(-0.3) \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(-1,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{underestimate}.$",
